# Vocab imports (always apply)

When adding vocabulary from notes, photos, PDFs, or similar for this repo:

1. **Translate missing sides — never skip for incomplete notes.**  
   If the user wrote only English, or only Japanese/Korean/Chinese (or only a reading), they do not know the other side. Translate it and **add the card anyway**. Target language → `term`, English → `meaning`.

2. **Skip duplicates; group similar words.**  
   Match on the target-language term (ignore readings in parentheses and spacing). If it already exists, skip.  
   When notes have many similar words, shades, or related phrases, **group them on one card** (main form in `term`, cluster + example in `notes`) instead of many near-duplicate cards. Keep truly different senses separate.  
   **Exception — Chinese colors:** each color (粉色、紫色、灰色, etc.) and 浅 / 深 get their own flashcard; do not cluster them.

3. **Every new card must have audio.**  
   Generate TTS for `term`, upload via `/api/media/upload`, set `audioKey` + `audioSource: "tts"`. Never leave newly added cards silent.  
   For short Japanese katakana loanwords, use hiragana TTS text and about `-20%` rate so it does not sound rushed.

4. **Every card gets a simple example + usage context.**  
   Put in `notes`: (1) one short target-language example sentence, (2) English translation on the next line, (3) brief usage context when the gloss alone is ambiguous (register, near-synonyms, “not X”, collocations). Never leave provenance-only text like `From sticky notes.` as the example — replace it, keeping any useful disambiguation.

4b. **Phrases get a word breakdown.**  
   For multi-word expressions / idioms / phrases, add a `Breakdown:` line after the translation glossing each content word (and important particles), e.g. `Breakdown: 귀가 = ears · 어둡다 = to be dark`. Single dictionary words do not need this.

4c. **Japanese multi-kanji terms get a kanji breakdown.**  
   When the Japanese term has two or more kanji, add `Breakdown:` glossing each kanji (e.g. `機 = opportunity/machine · 会 = meet`). Add a short usage-context line when notes are example-only.

5. **Japanese examples use hiragana furigana, never romaji.**  
   After harder kanji in example sentences, add `（ひらがな）` — e.g. `面白（おもしろ）い`. Do not transliterate the whole sentence into romaji (`Nihongo ga…`). Do not put English romaji in parentheses. Put the English translation on the following line.

5b. **Chinese cards get pinyin (reading + notes).**  
   Set `reading` to tone-marked pinyin for every Chinese card (spaced syllables). Keep `／` separators when the term is a grouped cluster.  
   In `notes`: add a full pinyin line under each Chinese example sentence, and `(pinyin)` after Chinese in Breakdown/context lines — the learner may not read characters yet.

6. Prefer the live cloud store so cards appear in study immediately. English on the front; target language on the back.